  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-1.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the two curves by setting the functions equal to one another and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit of integration are $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, consider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$,  and its projection on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215763/assets/0c2a676ecc781a2a7a78870eb93ee9fbe5a89a7bd754a446231c6e3237f75831.png)\n\nThe base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =|f(x)-g(x)| \\\\  & =|1-{x}^{2}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|2-{x}^{2}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of a rectangle is\n$A=bh.$\n  So, since we have $h=2b,$ the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(x) & =bh \\\\  & =2{b}^{2} \\\\  & =2{(2-{x}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}A(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
